# .gitignore — Couchbase Admin MCP Server
#
# The principle: this repository holds the SHIPPED SERVER and everything needed to
# build, run, test, document and audit it. What stays out is either reproducible
# (caches, build output, generated bytecode), private (credentials, live-org probe
# output), or a working copy of something already tracked (.bak files).
#
# Written from a full walk of the tree, then verified with
# `git check-ignore -v $(git ls-files)` returning nothing — i.e. no rule here hides
# a file the repository actually needs.
#
# TWO THINGS THIS FILE CANNOT DO:
#   * It does not untrack anything. A file already in the index stays tracked no
#     matter what is written here; that needs `git rm --cached <path>` once.
#   * It does not remove anything from history. A secret that was ever committed is
#     still in the history after being ignored.

# ── Python bytecode and build output ─────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
build/
dist/
wheels/
*.egg-info/
*.egg
.eggs/

# Packaged distributions built in the tree. The release artifact belongs on a
# GitHub release, not in git history.
#
# couchbase-admin-mcp-server.zip specifically: it is the ORIGINAL upstream snapshot
# this work started from, and it contains its own .git directory — tracking it would
# nest a repository inside this one and confuse both tooling and any reader. The
# history it holds is already here; this repository descends from those commits.
*.zip
*.whl
*.tar.gz

# ── Tool caches and coverage data ────────────────────────────────────────────
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.tox/
.nox/
htmlcov/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
cov.txt

# ── Virtual environments ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/

# ── Credentials and local configuration ──────────────────────────────────────
# .env.example IS tracked, deliberately: it is the documentation of every knob this
# server reads, and CI has no other description of them. A real .env never is.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# The operator env-script pattern (cbenv.ps1 / cbenv.bat / cbenv.sh). These hold
# live Capella API key SECRETS and cluster passwords in plain text.
cbenv.ps1
cbenv.bat
cbenv.sh

# Key material of any kind. mTLS client certs, the CA bundle for a self-signed
# self-managed cluster, KMIP material — all of it is per-deployment and none of it
# is ours to distribute.
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.cer
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks

# ── Runtime output ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The per-level rotating files this server writes, and the audit sink. The audit log
# is the one output that must never be committed: it records real bucket names, real
# usernames and real operations against a customer's cluster.
*.log
*.log.*
cb_admin_mcp*.log*
audit.log*
logs/

# ── Backup and staging files ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The .bak-<timestamp> convention gets used routinely when editing in place. Those
# are working copies of tracked files, so committing them doubles the file and
# invites someone editing the wrong one.
*.bak
*.bak-*
*.orig
*.rej
*.tmp
*~

# _to_delete/ is the staging area used because the remote device bridge cannot
# delete files: superseded files are moved there for manual removal.
_to_delete/
tracked_files_list.txt

# Drafts of this file. Once one is adopted it becomes .gitignore.
.gitignore.proposed

# ── Internal working documents, decided not to publish ───────────────────────
# CAPELLA_HANDOFF.md          engineering handoff: v4 path provenance and what is deferred
# SECURITY_AND_BUG_SCAN_...   the adversarial-scan writeup
# SECURITY_SCAN.md            the FIRST scan, whose verdict no longer holds
#
# The first two are internal records rather than documentation, and a public repository is
# not where either belongs.
#
# SECURITY_SCAN.md comes out for a different and stronger reason: its "no vulnerabilities"
# verdict was contradicted by the later scan, which found two security and two correctness
# defects on reachable paths. A superseded clean bill of health is worse than no document —
# a reader has no way to tell it is stale, and it is the one file in the repository that
# could be quoted back as an assurance nobody should rely on.
#
# What replaces all three for a reader: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md and section 9 of
# docs/CB_Admin_MCP_Architecture.docx for the controls and the verification state — which
# is dated, and says what was measured rather than pronouncing a verdict;
# handlers/capella/spec_pending.py for why each parked v4 path is parked; and
# docs/FIXTURE_DESIGN.md for the fixture layer.
#
# BOTH CAPELLA_HANDOFF.md AND SECURITY_SCAN.md WERE TRACKED before this decision, so these
# rules alone do nothing to them:
#     git rm --cached CAPELLA_HANDOFF.md SECURITY_SCAN.md
# is needed once, and both remain in the history of any clone made before that commit.
CAPELLA_HANDOFF.md
SECURITY_AND_BUG_SCAN_*.md
SECURITY_SCAN.md

# ── Live-organisation probe output and the probes themselves ─────────────────
# scripts/probe_pending.py and scripts/probe_pass2.py answer "does this v4 path
# exist?" against a REAL Capella organisation, and both the scripts and their output
# carry org, project and cluster ids. The durable conclusions belong in the internal
# handoff record and in spec.py's LIVE_VERIFIED set, not in a loose text file.
#
# scripts/verify_capella_paths.py — the reusable, credential-free path checker CI
# runs — IS tracked. If you decide the one-off probes are worth shipping, delete the
# two script lines below rather than force-adding them, so the decision is visible.
scripts/probe_pending.py
scripts/probe_pass2.py
probe.txt
probe.json
pass2.txt
pass2.json
smoke-report.json
smoke-indexes-*.json
verify_capella_paths-*.json

# ── Documentation: what ships and what does not ──────────────────────────────
# TRACKED, deliberately, because a reader needs the architecture and the install
# instructions without building anything:
#
#   docs/ARCHITECTURE.md                  the architecture, in text
#   docs/CB_Admin_MCP_Architecture.docx   the same plus per-interface setup steps
#   docs/ARCHITECTURE Deck.pptx           the walkthrough deck
#   docs/FIXTURE_DESIGN.md                the fixture layer's design notes
#   docs/diagrams/*.png                   the figures the documents embed
#   docs/diagrams-src/*.mmd               the Mermaid sources for arch01-08
#   docs/make_arch_diagrams.py            regenerates fig01-10 (matplotlib)
#   docs/build_architecture.js            builds the .docx from scratch (docx-js)
#   README.md                             quick start, Docker, Claude Desktop config
#
# The two generators are tracked so the documents can be REBUILT rather than only
# edited — a binary nobody can regenerate rots.
#
# Ignored below: the one-off patch scripts written to fix a specific defect in a
# specific build of the .docx (a page break landing wrong, one figure in the wrong
# place). They are not reusable, they do not describe the current document, and
# reading them would mislead someone into thinking they are part of the build.
docs/patch_architecture*.py
docs/move_figure2.py
docs/fix_fig2_pagebreak.py
docs/unbox_target_note.py
docs/_build/
docs/page-*.jpg
docs/page-*.png
docs/*.pdf

# ── Node ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used only by the console's compile-and-render tests and the .docx build.
node_modules/
package-lock.json
npm-debug.log*

# The vendored browser libraries under gui/static/vendor/ ARE tracked on purpose:
# the operator console must work on a host with no internet access, and
# tests/test_gui_frontend.py asserts they are present. Nothing above matches them —
# this note exists so nobody "tidies up" a vendor directory that is load-bearing.

# ── Editor and OS noise ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
